{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "With fixed capacitance, the charging law has a single RC timescale so dV_C_dt decreases with V_C mainly through one affine charging term plus the existing leakage nonlinearity.",
      "child_relation": "Let capacitance depend on capacitor voltage, so the same source_voltage and resistance produce a voltage-dependent effective charging timescale; dV_C_dt is reduced more strongly in the voltage range where capacitance increases, changing the curvature of V_C(t).",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "V_C",
        "capacitance"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across experiments varying source_voltage while holding resistance, nominal low-bias capacitance, and leakage parameters fixed, the rise is relatively slower in the higher-V_C portion of the trajectory for larger source_voltage because the effective capacitance grows with voltage; a single fixed-capacitance parent cannot match both low-voltage and higher-voltage trajectory curvature with one shared parameter set."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "rc_drive",
        "expression": "(source_voltage - V_C) / resistance",
        "role": "Available series charging current before division by effective capacitance"
      },
      {
        "name": "leak_activation",
        "expression": "Abs(V_C) / leakage_voltage_scale",
        "role": "Dimensionless regime indicator for leakage turn-on"
      },
      {
        "name": "normalized_voltage",
        "expression": "V_C / source_voltage",
        "role": "Position along the charging trajectory when source_voltage > 0"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when capacitor voltage spans a range over which real capacitance variation is non-negligible, e.g. higher source_voltage drives V_C into a regime where differential capacitance changes appreciably while resistance and leakage remain fixed.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, modest source_voltage variation may show slight late-time curvature changes; in holdout with larger source_voltage but same component family, the voltage-dependent capacitance produces a stronger slowdown at higher V_C that extends the same mechanism rather than introducing a new one.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent by taking the capacitance to be voltage-independent over the sampled range, i.e. the capacitance-variation amplitude tends to zero.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model treats capacitance as constant, omitting the common dielectric effect that a real capacitor's differential capacitance can vary with applied voltage.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The resistor and capacitor are ideal and time-invariant.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Voltage-dependent capacitance of the capacitor dielectric alters the effective RC timescale during charging.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "V_C",
      "capacitance",
      "source_voltage",
      "resistance"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the constant-capacitance assumption rather than add an unrelated new branch. The parent target mapping effectively uses one fixed storage factor capacitance, so for fixed resistance the charging slope at a given V_C is constrained to one shared RC scaling apart from the already modeled leakage activation. Vary source_voltage across runs, hold resistance and leakage parameters fixed, and observe V_C(t) from the same initial condition V_C(0)=0. In the parent, after refitting one global parameter set, trajectories at different source_voltage remain tied to the same capacitance and therefore to the same basic curvature pattern versus normalized progress, aside from leakage. In the child, if capacitance increases with |V_C|, higher-source runs enter a larger-capacitance regime later in the rise and show extra slowdown near the upper part of the trajectory. This is a changed target mapping because dV_C_dt itself at the observed state V_C changes as a function of V_C through the effective capacitance, not merely an auxiliary hidden trajectory. The effect is admissible when the applied voltage range is large enough for capacitance variation to matter; if the capacitor technology is nearly ideal over the sampled range, the refinement may be weak and the parent is recovered."
  },
